.TH BUFFER_MMAPPRIVATE_FD 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
buffer_mmapprivate_fd \- Map a file descriptor to a private memory region

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/buffer.h>
.IB #include <lib/mmap.h>

.IB ssize_t
.IB buffer_dummyreadmmap (fd_type fd, void* buf, size_t len, buffer* b);

.IB void
.IB buffer_munmap (buffer* b);

.IB int
.IB buffer_mmapprivate_fd (buffer* b, fd_type fd);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbuffer_mmapprivate_fd\fR function maps a file descriptor \fBfd\fR to a private memory region and initializes a buffer with it. The buffer is initialized with the \fBbuffer_dummyreadmmap\fR function as the read function and the \fBbuffer_munmap\fR function as the deinit function.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB buffer*
A pointer to the buffer structure.
.IB fd_type
The file descriptor to be mapped.

The function returns 0 on success, or -1 if an error occurred.

The \fBbuffer_mmapprivate_fd\fR function is intended to be used to map a file descriptor to a private memory region, such as when reading data from a file into a buffer for processing.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbuffer_mmapprivate_fd\fR function to map a file descriptor to a private memory region:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/buffer.h>
#include <lib/mmap.h>

int main() {
    buffer* b = buffer_new();
    fd_type fd = open("example.txt", O_RDONLY, 0644);
    if(fd < 0) {
        perror("open");
        buffer_free(b);
        return 1;
    }

    ssize_t result = buffer_mmapprivate_fd(b, fd);
    if(result == -1) {
        perror("buffer_mmapprivate_fd");
        close(fd);
        buffer_free(b);
        return 1;
    }

    close(fd);
    buffer_free(b);
    return 0;
}
.nef